INTRO(1)                       P32W Unix                        INTRO(1)

NAME
       intro - introduction to the P32W Unix manual

DESCRIPTION
       P32W Unix is a small multi-user Unix in the style of Version 6
       and Version 7, running on the P32W, a 32-bit CPU built from
       discrete TTL. Two people can use it at once, one on each
       serial line (/dev/tty0, the console, and /dev/tty1); each
       process runs in its own protected address space of 8 MiB;
       the root filesystem lives on a CompactFlash card.

       This manual describes the system as it is. Each page is a
       file of plain text; man(1) shows it. The manual is divided
       into sections:

       1      Commands: the programs in /bin, run from the shell.
       2      System calls: the entries into the kernel, as called
              from C (open(2), fork(2), ...).
       3      The C library: the functions in /usr/lib/libc.a and
              their headers in /usr/include (stdio(3), string(3)).
       5      File formats: /etc/passwd, crontab, the a.out format,
              the filesystem's layout on disk (passwd(5), fs(5)).
       8      System administration: the programs in /etc, and how
              the system starts (rc(8), init(8), mount(8)).

       A reference such as ls(1) means the page ls in section 1;
       man 1 ls shows it. Each section has an intro page (intro(2),
       intro(3)) that lists what it contains.

       THE PARTS OF A PAGE
       NAME           the name(s) the page describes and one line
                      about them; man -k searches these lines.
       SYNOPSIS       how to call it: words in [brackets] are
                      optional, ... means "repeat as often as you
                      like".
       DESCRIPTION    what it does.
       OPTIONS        each option in turn.
       EXAMPLES       typical uses.
       FILES          files it reads or writes.
       EXIT STATUS    what $? is afterwards: 0 means success.
       SEE ALSO       related pages.
       BUGS           known limits, and differences from the
                      Unix manuals you may know.

GETTING STARTED
       Log in as one of root, ken or guest (see login(1)); the shell
       sh(1) then prints a prompt and runs your commands. A few to
       begin with:

       ls             list the files here (ls -l: in detail)
       cd dir         go to another directory
       cat file       show a file (more file: a screen at a time)
       vi file        edit a file (or ed(1), the line editor)
       cc -o p p.c    compile a C program
       passwd         change your password
       who            who is logged in
       man -k word    find the pages about word
       exit           log out (or ^D)

       ^C interrupts a command, ^\ quits it, ^U erases the line.

SEE ALSO
       man(1), sh(1), login(1), intro(2), intro(3), rc(8)

BUGS
       There is no section 4 (devices) or 6 (games) or 7. The
       devices are /dev/tty0 and /dev/tty1 (the serial lines),
       /dev/tty (your own terminal), /dev/null, /dev/mem, /dev/port,
       /dev/disk (the filesystem area of the card), /dev/rdisk (the
       whole card, raw) and /dev/ram (a 256-block RAM disk).
